From d3f3faf3d8abe4745cb1c37245038816d583f98b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:44:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, windowing/gbm: Dolby Vision as HDR10+ chosen for
 a video, and as plain HDR10

Asked by a user who wants to compare HDR10 and HDR10+ from the player. In
1.0 RC6, "Dolby Vision output (this video)" offered "Dolby Vision as
HDR10", and whether the rebuilt picture then went out as HDR10+ (dynamic
metadata measured from it) was decided only by "Send it as HDR10+ where
the display takes it" (videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10plus): in a user's log,
"Dolby Vision as HDR10" chosen for a video went out as HDR10+ because
that setting was on, and there was no way to have the other one for one
video.

Two values of the list, both never native and rebuilding every stream as
"Dolby Vision as HDR10" did (OutputChoice::REBUILT_HDR10 = 6,
REBUILT_HDR10PLUS = 7; RebuiltChoice() in ConversionSettingFor()):

- "Dolby Vision as HDR10" (6): plain HDR10, whatever the setting says;
- "Dolby Vision as HDR10+" (7, #39829, Estuary #32427): HDR10+, whatever
  the setting says; offered only where the display takes HDR10+ (its
  block and the connector's HDR10PLUS_VSIF), the picture can be rebuilt
  for HDR10, and the settings do not map it to the display, which never
  sends HDR10+ (RebuiltHdr10PlusOffered()). Where it is not offered, no
  choice can send HDR10+, so "Dolby Vision as HDR10" is then what either
  would give.

"As the settings say" follows the settings as before. One rule decides
for every rebuilt picture (RebuiltHdr10PlusOf(): the choice of the
video, then the setting, the display and the mapping), in the only place
that decides it, CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::DVConversionHDR10Plus(), once
a video and again when the user chooses another output
(DVConversionChoiceChanged(), from SetOutputChoice(): the conversion may
go on across the reopening of the stream). DVPlusPossible(), which has
profile 8.1 rebuilt for HDR10+ by the setting, does not need the choice:
both choices rebuild every stream.

The choice kept for a file by 1.0 RC6 (1, HDR10) still loads and does
what it did: rebuilt, HDR10 or HDR10+ as the setting says. It is not in
the list any more; the list shows it as the value that gives what it
gives now (ListedOutputChoiceOf(): "as HDR10+" where that is offered and
the setting is on, else "as HDR10"), and choosing that value again
changes nothing. The memory of each file takes 6 and 7 (MAX_CHOICE 7); a
text of 1.0 RC6 reads as it was written. Read by 1.0 RC6, a line of 6
or 7 is left out, and gone when it writes the text again.

The log says, once a video and after each choice, what goes out and who
decided it ("DVBridge: Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10 goes out as HDR10+,
as the user chose for this video ..." / "... as HDR10, as the settings
say: ... is off ...", and where HDR10+ was chosen and cannot be had, why);
before, nothing was logged with the setting off. The names of the
choices in "the user chose for this video" and in the memory lines say
which is which. The player information follows a change of the choice
(PublishDVInfo() when what was sent is forgotten).

The help of the setting (#40927, Estuary #32101 in English and the three
Spanish) says that the video settings of the player can send one video
as plain HDR10 or as HDR10+. The values of the list have no help of
their own, as the others.

Tested on the host: the test of the rules (rebuilt_hdr10plus,
every choice against the rule of 1.0 RC6, the offer against what a
choice really sends, the list, the rules of the stream in every
combination, the memory) and the copies of dvchoice_test and
dvplus_policy_test with the two values; they do not build against the code before this change, and seven mutations of the headers make them fail. The
thirteen earlier tests that include DVPlaybackPolicy.h give the same
results before and after this change. Syntax: the 16 sources that the build
compiles with these files, with the build's commands. Not run: the list,
the reopening and the InfoFrame on a display.
